  "note": "Run on 2026-10-17 and 2026-10-18 on the live AwwO, in the operator’s own account and workspace, on Claude Opus 4.8, every agent working in its own sandbox computer. The first run of the canvas finished 7 of 13 nodes: two builders (Selection, Panels) used up their 64 model calls and one (Painting) stopped on a model error. Five more runs on the same canvas then ran only what had not finished, keeping what had: in the second the three builders and the assembly finished and the integration tester used up its model calls; the tester’s task was rewritten before each of the next three, it used up its calls again in the third and stopped on a model error after 19 minutes in the fourth, and it finished in the fifth; the sixth ran only the review again, which in the fifth had wrapped its report in a JSON block after a sentence (the worker now reads that). The spec wrapped its spec the same way in the first run, before that fix, and the builders read it so: it shows here as it was delivered. The sixth run’s page was published on 2026-10-17; on 2026-10-18 four faults were found in it (below), and the text of the three builders they came from was rewritten: the Kernel (the self-test changed the open document in place), the Interface (menus never hid, and dialogs called their buttons without the close function the filters’ dialogs expect) and the Filters (its task gave it the commands that add an adjustment layer, which the spec gives to the Files module). The seventh run fixed those three faults but its Interface showed empty dialogs, its Filters dropped every label and its tester used up its model calls. Runs 8 to 13 ran only builders, and the page their deliveries assemble to was opened in Chrome before each next run: the eighth ran the three again (dialogs and labels right, but the panels no longer followed the document), the ninth the Interface (the dock now draws its panels again as the document changes), the tenth the Kernel and the Interface with two more faults the same check found in the published page as well (tool keys selected nothing; the canvas menu ignored Escape), and the 11th to 13th the Kernel alone, each for what the one before left: a command listed twice under an id of its own (the tenth), test commands left in the Edit menu after a self-test (the 11th), nine commands that no module registered (the 12th). The 14th run assembled the page and ran the tester and the review; the tester’s task now allows five fixes, at most four model calls each, and names what the earlier runs found behind the failures the kept modules bring back, and it fixed four. A 15th run tried the Interface once more, to keep the panel its own test registers out of the dock; that page opened without its demo, so it was not kept: the record ends with the 14th run, and the canvas text is the one that run ran. Each node shows its last run, and every node’s text is the canvas a member copies today. The receipt names the revisions each node ran on; the runtime’s directories on the host are named after the same files’ commits before a rebase (2923df74, fc62b065 and e009cb32). The delivered page is the tester’s, exactly as delivered, with no manual edits.",
  "verification": "Before publishing, the delivered page was opened in Chrome (1440×900 and 1280×720) with every network request blocked and no script errors: it opens on the 1600 × 1000 “Dusk” demo, nine layers (two of them adjustment layers) listed in the layers panel, the image fitted to the stage and 1600 × 1000 px in the status bar. Its self-test passes 61 of its 63 tests and rejects all 20 planted failures, as the tester reported; the two failing tests are the ones its report names: “panels: bring forward moves layer up” expects the layer to stay where it was, which the core’s own move test contradicts, and “invert twice restores every pixel” builds its image with one argument where ImageData takes two. The four faults found in the page published before are gone: OK and Cancel in Gaussian Blur and in Hue/Saturation close the dialog without an error, and OK applies the filter as one history step; every menu closes when an item is picked and on Escape, the canvas menu too; each command is listed once (the Layer menu’s 12 “New …” entries once each); and the self-test, from the page or the Help menu, leaves the open document as it was. Also checked: B, E and the other tool keys pick their tools; the layers and properties panels follow a new layer, an adjustment layer and a picked layer; the opacity slider drags; the palette finds Undo from “und”; File › New makes a 1920 × 1080 document; a brush stroke is one history step and undoes. Adj in the layers panel adds a Hue/Saturation adjustment layer, and each click on its Hue slider re-colours the whole picture (the cover). One fault was seen: after a self-test, an empty “Probe” section stays at the top of the dock, the panel the Interface’s own test registers to watch the dock draw it."
